                ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-item", flag=true), attr("data-step", "2"), attr("aria-label", "select describes a new result")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgGroup, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "514"), attr("y", "238"), attr("width", "254"), attr("height", "122"), attr("rx", "12"), attr("fill", "#f7f0e5"), attr("stroke", "#b79662"), attr("stroke-width", "2")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Same name?", style="trial-title", attributes=[attr("x", "536"), attr("y", "272")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Rebind sales to", style="trial-note", attributes=[attr("x", "536"), attr("y", "306")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="the new result", style="trial-note", attributes=[attr("x", "536"), attr("y", "337")]),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-item", flag=true), attr("data-step", "3"), attr("aria-label", "Assignment changes a name, not the original DataFrame")]),
            ], attributes=[attr("viewBox", "0 0 1308 390"), attr("role", "img"), attr("aria-labelledby", "data-transform-title data-transform-desc")]),
            group([
                paragraph([
                    strong("Describe a new result"),
                    span("A transformation creates a new DataFrame description. An action will request execution."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-0"),
                paragraph([
                    strong("1 · Keep the original in view"),
                    span("sales refers to the original DataFrame, with category and amount."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-1"),
                paragraph([
                    strong("2 · Create a new description"),
                    span("amounts describes a projection of sales. The original stays unchanged; no full copy is implied."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-2"),
                paragraph([
                    strong("3 · What if we reuse the name?"),
                    span("sales = sales.select(\"amount\") rebinds sales to the new DataFrame. It does not edit the old one."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-3"),
            ], style="narration-cues"),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "prev")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Play slowly", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "play")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Pause", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "pause")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "next")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "replay")]),
                node(NodeKind.Span, children=[
                    text("Step "),
                    span("3", attributes=[attr("data-motion-step-label", flag=true)]),
                    text(" of 3"),
                ], style="core-step"),
            ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-controls", flag=true), attr("hidden", flag=true)]),
            node(NodeKind.Paragraph, style="sr-only", attributes=[attr("data-motion-status", flag=true), attr("role", "status"), attr("aria-live", "polite"), attr("aria-atomic", "true")]),
        ], style="core-motion jvm-walkthrough trial-motion", attributes=[attr("data-motion-root", flag=true), attr("data-motion-mode", "step"), attr("data-step-count", "3"), attr("data-step-current", "3"), attr("data-frame", "static"), attr("style", "--motion-hold:6000ms"), attr("tabindex", "0"), attr("aria-label", "Describe a new result. Use Next point to advance.")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("34"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""TRIAL NARRATIVE, 3 of 3. Read amounts = sales.select("amount") from right to left. “Take this description of sales. Describe a result with just amount. Give that new DataFrame the Python name amounts.” Pause and ask: “Have we processed those rows yet?” Answer: select itself is a transformation, not an action. The right-hand box deliberately shows a plan description rather than a completed output table. The left-hand rows illustrate the input, not data stored in the DataFrame object.
The third step is optional discussion: show the complete one-line form verbally, sales = sales.select("amount"). The callout describes rebinding in words. Assignment changes a Python binding, not the original DataFrame. The new plan still depends on the old input description. Immutability does not promise a snapshot of changing external source files. Finish with: “What causes Spark to actually do the work? That is where transformations and actions come in.” The original detailed slides follow during this trial so Danny can compare; the intended eventual transition is Lazy Pandas.
Source: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html""",
    )

pub def slide_core_abstractions() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="core-abstractions",
        title="Spark’s Data APIs",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide core-slide migrated",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Spark’s Data APIs", level=1),
            prose("Three interfaces to distributed data", style="subtitle"),
            group([
            group([
                strong("RDD"),
                span("Elements + functions"),
                prose("You describe operations on individual elements."),
            ]),
            group([
                strong("DataFrame"),
                span("Rows + schema + expressions"),
                prose("Spark can reason about columns and the query."),
            ]),
            group([
                strong("Dataset[T]"),
                span("Typed objects + Spark SQL"),
                prose("Scala and Java add a typed object interface."),
            ]),
        ], style="api-rows"),
            prose("For this course, we will mainly work with DataFrames.", style="takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("35"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Use the same three abstractions as the original API overview. Recall their evolution from the history: RDDs came first, DataFrames arrived in 1.3, typed Datasets in 1.6, and the APIs were unified in 2.0. This is not a claim that RDDs were introduced in Spark 1.0. Say: these are different ways to describe distributed data. Explain RDDs, place Datasets, then spend our time on DataFrames.  Original workshop slides 15–21. Updated against https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html and https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html . ",
    )

pub def slide_rdd_api() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="rdd-api",
        title="Resilient Distributed Dataset API",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide core-slide migrated",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Resilient Distributed Dataset API", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Figure, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Svg, children=[
                node(NodeKind.SvgTitle, content="RDDs and partitions", attributes=[attr("id", "rdd-partitions-title")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgDescription, content="A collection is split into partitions; tasks can process different partitions in parallel.", attributes=[attr("id", "rdd-partitions-desc")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgDefinitions, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgMarker, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgPath, attributes=[attr("d", "M0 0L8 3L0 6Z"), attr("fill", "#286faf")]),
                    ], attributes=[attr("id", "rdd-partitions-arrow"), attr("markerWidth", "8"), attr("markerHeight", "6"), attr("refX", "7"), attr("refY", "3"), attr("orient", "auto")]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgGroup, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "12"), attr("y", "12"), attr("width", "1280"), attr("height", "320"), attr("rx", "6"), attr("fill", "#f1f5f8"), attr("stroke", "#9badb9"), attr("stroke-width", "2")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="RDD · one distributed collection", style="core-svg-heading", attributes=[attr("x", "36"), attr("y", "52")]),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-item", flag=true), attr("data-step", "1"), attr("aria-label", "One collection, divided into partitions")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgGroup, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "40"), attr("y", "86"), attr("width", "380"), attr("height", "158"), attr("rx", "6"), attr("fill", "#e8f0f7"), attr("stroke", "#286faf"), attr("stroke-width", "2")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Partition 0", style="core-svg-heading", attributes=[attr("x", "60"), attr("y", "120")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="(\"books\", 25)", style="core-svg-code", attributes=[attr("x", "60"), attr("y", "164")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="(\"games\", 40)", style="core-svg-code", attributes=[attr("x", "60"), attr("y", "200")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "464"), attr("y", "86"), attr("width", "380"), attr("height", "158"), attr("rx", "6"), attr("fill", "#e8f0f7"), attr("stroke", "#286faf"), attr("stroke-width", "2")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Partition 1", style="core-svg-heading", attributes=[attr("x", "484"), attr("y", "120")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="(\"books\", 15)", style="core-svg-code", attributes=[attr("x", "484"), attr("y", "164")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="(\"music\", 10)", style="core-svg-code", attributes=[attr("x", "484"), attr("y", "200")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "888"), attr("y", "86"), attr("width", "380"), attr("height", "158"), attr("rx", "6"), attr("fill", "#e8f0f7"), attr("stroke", "#286faf"), attr("stroke-width", "2")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Partition 2", style="core-svg-heading", attributes=[attr("x", "908"), attr("y", "120")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="(\"games\", 30)", style="core-svg-code", attributes=[attr("x", "908"), attr("y", "164")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="(\"music\", 20)", style="core-svg-code", attributes=[attr("x", "908"), attr("y", "200")]),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-item", flag=true), attr("data-step", "2"), attr("aria-label", "Partitions can be processed in parallel")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgGroup, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Resilient: recover a lost partition by recomputing its lineage.", style="core-svg-note", attributes=[attr("x", "40"), attr("y", "292")]),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-item", flag=true), attr("data-step", "3"), attr("aria-label", "Lost partitions can be recomputed from lineage")]),
            ], attributes=[attr("viewBox", "0 0 1308 350"), attr("role", "img"), attr("aria-labelledby", "rdd-partitions-title rdd-partitions-desc")]),
            group([
                paragraph([
                    strong("RDDs and partitions"),
                    span("A collection is split into partitions; tasks can process different partitions in parallel."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-0"),
                paragraph([
                    strong("1 · Dataset"),
                    span("A collection of elements — here, Python tuples."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-1"),
                paragraph([
                    strong("2 · Distributed"),
                    span("Different tasks can work on different partitions in parallel."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-2"),
                paragraph([
                    strong("3 · Resilient"),
                    span("Spark tracks how partitions were derived, so lost data can be recomputed."),
                ], style="narration-cue cue-3"),
            ], style="narration-cues"),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "prev")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Play slowly", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "play")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Pause", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "pause")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "next")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "replay")]),
                node(NodeKind.Span, children=[
                    text("Step "),
                    span("3", attributes=[attr("data-motion-step-label", flag=true)]),
                    text(" of 3"),
                ], style="core-step"),
            ], attributes=[attr("data-motion-controls", flag=true), attr("hidden", flag=true)]),
            node(NodeKind.Paragraph, style="sr-only", attributes=[attr("data-motion-status", flag=true), attr("role", "status"), attr("aria-live", "polite"), attr("aria-atomic", "true")]),
        ], style="core-motion jvm-walkthrough", attributes=[attr("data-motion-root", flag=true), attr("data-motion-mode", "step"), attr("data-step-count", "3"), attr("data-step-current", "3"), attr("data-frame", "static"), attr("style", "--motion-hold:6000ms"), attr("tabindex", "0"), attr("aria-label", "RDDs and partitions. Use previous and next point to step through the explanation.")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("36"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Ask what the two tuple positions mean. We know category and amount, but this RDD has no SQL schema declaring those names. The displayed placement is conceptual, not a permanent partition-to-machine assignment. RDDs are immutable; lineage supports recovery, provided the required inputs remain available. In Python elements can be Python objects; do not describe all RDD elements as JVM objects. Do not repeat the old statement that RDDs require users to specify a schema. The spark.mllib maintenance statement was about the old ML library, not all RDDs.  Original workshop slides 15–21. Updated against https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html and https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html . ",
    )

pub def slide_dataset_api() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="dataset-api",
        title="Dataset API",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide core-slide migrated",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Dataset API", level=1),
            prose("Typed objects with Spark SQL’s execution engine", style="subtitle"),
            group([
            group([
                span("SCALA EXAMPLE", style="concept-label"),
                node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                    code("""case class Sale(category: String,
                amount: Long)

val sales: Dataset[Sale]"""),
                ]),
                paragraph([
                    text("Each element is exposed as a "),
                    code("Sale"),
                    text("."),
                ]),
            ], style="type-example"),
            group([
                heading("Objects at the API boundary", level=2),
                prose("Encoders map typed objects to Spark SQL’s internal representation."),
                heading("Types checked by the compiler", level=2),
                paragraph([
                    text("Available in Scala and Java."),
                    line_break(),
                    text("Python uses the DataFrame API."),
                ]),
            ], style="dataset-explanation"),
        ], style="dataset-layout"),
            paragraph([
            text("In Scala, "),
            code("DataFrame"),
            text(" is an alias for "),
            code("Dataset[Row]"),
            text("."),
        ], style="takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("37"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Keep this short as in the original workshop. The Scala snippet is a type illustration, not a complete runnable application. Typed operations offer compile-time checking; string-based column expressions can still fail at analysis/runtime, so avoid claiming every Dataset operation is compile-time safe. In Java the corresponding DataFrame representation is Dataset<Row>. There is no typed Dataset[T] API in PySpark. Bridge: what if our elements are rows and the structure is described by a schema?  Original workshop slides 15–21. Updated against https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html and https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html . ",
    )

pub def slide_dataframe_api() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="dataframe-api",
        title="DataFrame API",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide core-slide migrated",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("DataFrame API", level=1),
            prose("Distributed rows, organised into named columns", style="subtitle"),
            group([
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="category"),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="amount"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="books"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="25"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="games"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="40"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="books"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="15"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="music"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="10"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ], style="core-table"),
                prose("A logical table view — no guaranteed row order.", style="small-note"),
            ]),
            group([
                heading("The schema gives data meaning", level=2),
                group([
                    code("category"),
                    span("string"),
                ], style="schema-line"),
                group([
                    code("amount"),
                    span("long"),
                ], style="schema-line"),
                prose("Column names, types and expressions give Spark information it can use to optimise the query.", style="core-prose"),
            ]),
        ], style="df-layout"),
            code_block("sales.select(\"category\", \"amount\")"),
            prose("SQL and the DataFrame API use the same Spark SQL engine.", style="takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("38"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Use the same tuples as the RDD slide, now with named columns and types. Ask: what additional information does Spark have now? Schema may be supplied or inferred, depending on the source. A DataFrame is not a local pandas table and does not imply that all rows are in driver memory. Built-in expressions expose structure to the optimizer; do not say DataFrames are universally faster for every possible operation. Reading can do metadata/schema work even before a later action; the lazy section will explain the distinction.  Original workshop slides 15–21. Updated against https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html and https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html . ",
    )

pub def slide_dataframe_partitions() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="dataframe-partitions",
        title="A DataFrame is distributed",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide core-slide migrated",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
